/* ===== story-ataturk.js ===== */
/* More stories from his life (ataturk), grounded in recorded events or marked as tradition. */
IRON_TRAIN("ataturk", { stories: [
 "The watch at Gallipoli. In August 1915 at the Conkbayırı ridge, they say a piece of shrapnel struck my chest and was stopped by the pocket watch in my coat. I do not tell it to boast. I tell it because that morning the line was breaking, and I decided not to count my own odds but to hold the ridge. Some days the only useful thing is to stay where you are needed.",
 "The teacher in the park. In 1928 we changed the alphabet, from Arabic letters to Latin. It was one of the hardest reforms, because a nation had to learn to read again. I went to the people with a blackboard and taught in public places, and for months the whole country became a school. If a change is hard, do not only order it. Stand at the blackboard yourself.",
 "Samsun. In May 1919 I sailed to Samsun as an army inspector, officially sent to calm things. In truth I went to begin a movement to save the country. The Sultan's government wanted order; the nation needed resistance. I carried the first duty on paper and the second in my heart. Sometimes a great thing starts in the shadow of an ordinary job.",
 "The surface of defense. In August 1921, as the Greek army approached Ankara, I took command of the army. I told the officers there is no line of defense, only a surface of defense, and that surface is the whole homeland. Every piece of ground must be defended until it is soaked with blood. The Sakarya battle lasted twenty-two days. When there is no line to hold, you must make a nation the line.",
 "Forward, to the Mediterranean. On 1 September 1922 I wrote to the army: 'Armies, your first target is the Mediterranean. Forward!' Within days, the Greek front collapsed and Izmir was reached. A clear target, stated in a short sentence, can move a hundred thousand men. Say what you want in a way that a tired soldier can repeat.",
 "The Speech. In October 1927 I read a speech to the Assembly that lasted about thirty-six hours across six days. I told the whole story of how the nation was saved, from Samsun to the Republic. I wanted the next generation to know it from the one who had lived it. If you have been part of something great, write it down while you can.",
 "The farm. In the 1920s I bought a bare, stony piece of land outside Ankara, and with engineers and workers, I made a model farm and forest. They planted trees where there had been dust. I wanted to show that this land could grow. Teach by doing. A forest in the middle of a steppe says more than a speech."
] });
;

/* ===== story-atsiz.js ===== */
/* More stories from his life (atsiz), grounded in recorded events or marked as tradition. */
IRON_TRAIN("atsiz", { stories: [
 "The name. I took the name Atsız, which means 'nameless', and I chose it to say that a man is measured by his deeds, not by a title or a family name. When people asked what it meant, I told them: let my work carry the name. If your work is good, it will need no introduction.",
 "The trial of 1944. In 1944 I was put on trial for my political views, and I spent time in prison. I did not change what I wrote, and I did not beg. Whether you agree with me or not, remember this: a person who is ready to pay for his words has a different weight from one who only shouts them.",
 "The novel of the steppe. In the 1940s I wrote Bozkurtların Ölümü, a story about the old Göktürks and their struggle. I wrote it because history has to be felt, not only read. Young readers took it to heart. A good story can carry a nation's memory farther than a hundred lectures.",
 "The sailor's son. I was born in Istanbul in 1905, the son of a naval officer. We were a family of soldiers and sailors, and I grew up with stories of ships and of the old state. I never forgot that a person comes from somewhere. Know your roots, and do not apologize for them.",
 "Orhun. In the 1930s I edited a magazine called Orhun, named after the valley where the oldest Turkish inscriptions stand. It was small and not always loved. But it was mine, and it said what I believed. If you have something to say, make a place where you can say it, even if it is tiny."
] });
;

